WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.331
Crypto agility is the ability to change cryptographic algorithms, keys, libraries,

00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:07.024
and protocols without redesigning an entire system.

00:00:07.024 --> 00:00:10.871
It is not a slogan; it is an architectural property.

00:00:10.871 --> 00:00:13.178
A crypto-agile system avoids hard-coded assumptions,

00:00:13.178 --> 00:00:16.640
keeps algorithms configurable where appropriate, supports versioned protocol negotiation,

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.179
separates policy from implementation,

00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:23.564
and gives teams a realistic way to test and deploy changes without emergency rewrites.

00:00:23.564 --> 00:00:25.103
Prioritization should be risk-based.

00:00:25.103 --> 00:00:29.719
Systems protecting sensitive data with a long confidentiality lifetime deserve early attention,

00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:35.874
especially if they are exposed to untrusted networks or depend on public-key encryption and key establishment.

00:00:35.874 --> 00:00:42.028
Operationally critical systems also need careful planning because outages can be more damaging than delayed migration.

00:00:42.028 --> 00:00:43.951
Dependency complexity matters as well.

00:00:43.951 --> 00:00:49.337
A service may be ready internally but blocked by a client library, partner integration,

00:00:49.337 --> 00:00:52.029
hardware module, certificate authority, or vendor product.

00:00:52.029 --> 00:00:54.722
Hybrid approaches may appear during the transition.

00:00:54.722 --> 00:00:55.372
Conceptually,

00:00:55.372 --> 00:00:59.988
a hybrid design combines an existing mechanism with a post-quantum mechanism so

00:00:59.988 --> 00:01:04.219
the system can manage interoperability and risk while the ecosystem matures.

00:01:04.219 --> 00:01:07.681
Hybrid does not mean automatically safer in every implementation,

00:01:07.681 --> 00:01:11.143
and it does not remove the need for testing.

00:01:11.143 --> 00:01:16.528
It is a migration strategy that must be evaluated in the context of protocols,

00:01:16.528 --> 00:01:19.221
libraries, performance, failure behavior, and operational visibility.

00:01:19.221 --> 00:01:22.298
A practical plan uses pilots before broad rollout.

00:01:22.298 --> 00:01:25.760
Teams test interoperability, measure performance, review logging, validate monitoring,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.222
confirm certificate and key lifecycle behavior, coordinate with vendors,

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:30.761
and define fallback criteria.

00:01:30.761 --> 00:01:35.761
The danger is a rushed replacement that breaks systems or silently weakens security.

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:38.839
A disciplined migration creates decision records, tracks readiness,

00:01:38.839 --> 00:01:42.420
and moves high-risk use cases forward while keeping production stable.
